\chapter{Cartesian Kerr--Schild Manifold and Horizon Regularization}
\label{ch:kerr_schild}

\section{Singularity Invariants and Boyer--Lindquist Coordinate Breakdown}
In standard Boyer--Lindquist coordinates $(t, r, \theta, \phi)$, the Kerr metric exhibits coordinate divergence at the outer event horizon $r_+ = M + \sqrt{M^2 - a^2}$ where the metric function $\Delta(r) = r^2 - 2Mr + a^2 \to 0$. Numerical integrators evaluating null geodesics across $r \to r_+$ encounter catastrophic floating-point overflow:

\begin{equation}
    ds^2 = -\left(1 - \frac{2Mr}{\Sigma}\right) dt^2 - \frac{4aMr\sin^2\theta}{\Sigma} dt\,d\phi + \frac{\Sigma}{\Delta} dr^2 + \Sigma\, d\theta^2 + \left(r^2 + a^2 + \frac{2a^2Mr\sin^2\theta}{\Sigma}\right)\sin^2\theta\, d\phi^2
\end{equation}

\section{Cartesian Kerr--Schild Formulation}
To establish an everywhere-regular framework, we map the manifold onto Minkowski space plus an outer product of null vectors:
\begin{equation}
    g_{\mu\nu} = \eta_{\mu\nu} + 2 H k_\mu k_\nu, \qquad g^{\mu\nu} = \eta^{\mu\nu} - 2 H k^\mu k^\nu
\end{equation}
where the scalar field $H$ and contravariant null congruence $k^\mu$ are given by:
\begin{equation}
    H(x, y, z) = \frac{M r^3}{r^4 + a^2 z^2}, \quad k^\mu = \left(-1, \frac{rx + ay}{r^2 + a^2}, \frac{ry - ax}{r^2 + a^2}, \frac{z}{r}\right)
\end{equation}
The auxiliary radius $r$ is solved explicitly via the quartic root:
\begin{equation}
    r^2 = \frac{1}{2}\left( R^2 - a^2 + \sqrt{(R^2 - a^2)^2 + 4a^2 z^2} \right), \quad R^2 = x^2 + y^2 + z^2
\end{equation}

